  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0004] Exclude bool when validating integer fields: When validating integer arguments in ledgerly, check isinstance(x, bool) first, since bool is a subclass of int and True would otherwise pass a positive-integer check.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary results with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 1x, 1 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0002] Normalize money via to_money and round_money: In ledgerly/invoice.py, convert any incoming price with to_money() and store it rounded with round_money(); compare against ZERO from ledgerly.money rather than a literal Decimal.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with stable snake_case codes: Reject invalid input by raising BillingError(code, detail) from ledgerly.errors with the established codes such as \"invalid_state\" and \"negative_amount\", passing repr() of the offending value as detail.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)",
